2486 0 obj
<</Count 1/Dest[39 0 R/XYZ 112 592 null]/First 2487 0 R/Last 2487 0 R/Next 2488 0 R/Parent 1958 0 R/Prev 2485 0 R/SE 1087 0 R/Title(This Article argues that passing unenforceable regulations is a significant regulatory oversight . Once regulations are enacted, stakeholders exploit drafting and enforcement weaknesses to their advantage, resulting in market failures such as decreases in affordable housing and inability to enforce against illegal STR listings .This phenomenon is known as regulatory arbitrage .Regardless of whether it harms or helps government, business, or society, regulatory arbitrage is predictable . Local governments ha)>>
endobj

2487 0 obj
<</Dest[39 0 R/XYZ 154 527 null]/Parent 2486 0 R/SE 1088 0 R/Title(159 )>>
endobj

2488 0 obj
<</Count 2/Dest[39 0 R/XYZ 112 423 null]/First 2489 0 R/Last 2490 0 R/Next 2491 0 R/Parent 1958 0 R/Prev 2486 0 R/SE 1089 0 R/Title(Without a strong enforcement identity, local governments sway between the arguments for and against STRs during the regulatory process, weakening their resolve in the face of stakeholder pressures . This has been termed the \215Goldilocks Regulatory Challenge,\216 where the aspiration to create laws that are \215just right\216\204neither too broad \(overinclusive\) nor too narrow \(underinclusive\)\204results in underinclusive laws filled with loopholes ripe for regulatory arbitrage . Local governments passed STR regulations to )>>
endobj

